Experiment 4: Matrix ADT Operations
Start the program.
Create a class Matrix with row, col and a 10×10 array a.
readMatrix() reads the order and the elements. displayMatrix() prints them row by row, separated by tabs.
Addition: if the rows and columns of both matrices are equal, set temp[i][j] = a[i][j] + m.a[i][j]. Otherwise print “not possible”.
Subtraction: use the same condition, with temp[i][j] = a[i][j] - m.a[i][j].
Multiplication: if col of the first equals row of the second, then for each i and j:
Set temp[i][j] = 0.
For k = 0..col-1, do temp[i][j] += a[i][k] * m.a[k][j].
The result order is row × m.col.
isValid() returns true only if the result has row > 0 and col > 0.
In main(), read A and B, display them, then perform and show the three operations.
Stop.



Experiment 5: Constructor & Destructor Order in Multiple Inheritance
Start the program.
Create a base class Student (with rollNo) and a base class Sports (with sportsMarks). Each has a default constructor, a parameterized constructor, a copy constructor, and a destructor that prints a message.
Create class Result : public Student, public Sports with a totalMarks member.
Write Result’s default, parameterized and copy constructors using an initializer list (: Student(r), Sports(s)), plus a destructor.
display() prints rollNo, sportsMarks and totalMarks.
In main(), create r1 (default), r2(101, 25, 450) (parameterized) and r3(r2) (copy), and display each.
When main() ends, the objects are destroyed in reverse order (r3, r2, r1). Within each object the order is Result, then Sports, then Student.
Stop.



